WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:07.480
Your sheets arrived finished. Every mark was already there. So where did the marks come from?

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:12.260
Somebody counted. And it's a job you can do yourselves.

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:21.520
Start with a text. Lowercase everything. And notice that full stops and exclamation marks are words too—they get their own rows.

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:40.420
Read it two words at a time. The first word names the row. The second word gets a mark in that row. Then move along by one: the second word of this pair is the first word of the next. Read the text once, and every pair in it gets counted.

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:56.120
Keep going and the same pair comes round again. The second time the text says "the magpie," the mark goes beside magpie in the row for the, which already has one. That's how a count builds. Common pairs collect marks; rare ones don't.

00:00:56.630 --> 00:01:07.880
Check it against your printed sheet. The magpie, five marks. Count the book and it's five. Every sheet in this room was made exactly this way.

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:23.260
Now it's your turn, on a new text, with blank sheets. This time the words are yours to write as well as the marks. One person reads the text two words at a time. Whoever has the row for the first word puts a mark beside the second.

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:47.180
Three rules keep your model honest. When you start a row for a new word, say the word out loud, so nobody else starts one too—two people counting the same word splits the model in half. A new word goes in the next empty box, never a skipped one—the colours go in order. And the row for full stop is a real row: it says how sentences start.

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:56.180
When you've read the whole text, generate from it. Start from the. It should sound like your text, and nobody else's.

00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:04.360
That's training. Not a computer reading the internet. Somebody counting what came next, a lot.

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:09.420
Reader, grab the text. Two words at a time.
